00:28:32.079 --> 00:28:36.079
Love each other as I have loved you.

00:28:36.079 --> 00:28:38.079
And greater love has no one than this

00:28:38.079 --> 00:28:43.079
to lay down one's life for one's friends.

00:28:43.079 --> 00:28:45.079
I went to really a church for many years.

00:28:45.079 --> 00:28:46.079
It was extremely formative for me,

00:28:46.079 --> 00:28:48.079
like pretty much all my 20s,

00:28:48.079 --> 00:28:49.079
half my 30s I was there,

00:28:49.079 --> 00:28:52.079
and even 30s part I was on staff at that church.

00:28:52.079 --> 00:28:56.079
And they had, like just far and away

00:28:56.079 --> 00:28:57.079
the highest value at this church

00:28:57.079 --> 00:29:00.079
was relationships and community.

00:29:00.079 --> 00:29:02.079
It was just like an obsession almost.

00:29:02.079 --> 00:29:03.079
You know, like this is the thing.

00:29:03.079 --> 00:29:06.079
We love the term, we put the word real before it,

00:29:06.079 --> 00:29:08.079
so it's like all about real relationships.

00:29:08.079 --> 00:29:14.079
And by real, the idea was kind of authentic, right?

00:29:14.079 --> 00:29:16.079
Not surface level, not fake,

00:29:16.079 --> 00:29:19.079
but kind of a real, like real relationships.

00:29:19.079 --> 00:29:20.079
And of course by relationship

00:29:20.079 --> 00:29:22.079
it was referring a little bit more to friendship.

00:29:22.079 --> 00:29:24.079
We did talk a lot about marriage in that community,

00:29:24.079 --> 00:29:27.079
but when we use at least that phrase, real relationships,

00:29:27.079 --> 00:29:29.079
it really was something more than that.

00:29:29.079 --> 00:29:32.079
It was like friendships and the communal element.

00:29:32.079 --> 00:29:35.079
And I was really intrigued,

00:29:35.079 --> 00:29:38.079
like I wasn't born and raised in that church,

00:29:38.079 --> 00:29:40.079
so I came to it right in my,

00:29:40.079 --> 00:29:42.079
I mean I was still young, I was like 19 or so

00:29:42.079 --> 00:29:44.079
when I first went there,

00:29:44.079 --> 00:29:46.079
but still I saw it a little bit

00:29:46.079 --> 00:29:48.079
with an outsider's kind of view,

00:29:48.079 --> 00:29:51.079
and I was really intrigued by this like value

00:29:51.079 --> 00:29:52.079
and it's almost an obsession.

00:29:52.079 --> 00:29:55.079
I mean, you know, in a kind of good way though,

00:29:55.079 --> 00:29:57.079
like every other sermon was about this,

00:29:57.079 --> 00:30:00.079
like I've heard it said every preacher really has one message

00:30:00.079 --> 00:30:03.079
and they're just saying it like a thousand different ways.

00:30:03.079 --> 00:30:05.079
You know, that might be overly reductionistic,

00:30:05.079 --> 00:30:07.079
where, I don't know, maybe we have like,

00:30:07.079 --> 00:30:09.079
I probably have like five to seven themes,

00:30:09.079 --> 00:30:10.079
but I'm just hitting those things, you know,

00:30:10.079 --> 00:30:12.079
I'm just like doing it at different angles.

00:30:12.079 --> 00:30:13.079
And that was this kind of church, you know,

00:30:13.079 --> 00:30:15.079
like had that kind of like,

00:30:15.079 --> 00:30:18.079
this is the passion, this is the driving force, you know.

00:30:18.079 --> 00:30:20.079
And I think a lot of the reason for that

00:30:20.079 --> 00:30:21.079
was the founding pastor.

00:30:21.079 --> 00:30:25.079
So he founded that church in 1999,

00:30:25.079 --> 00:30:27.079
of course, you know, preached the majority of the sermons,

00:30:27.079 --> 00:30:29.079
so had a lot of influence in that way.

00:30:29.079 --> 00:30:31.079
He's still there to this day,

00:30:31.079 --> 00:30:34.079
and, you know, 25 whatever years later.

00:30:34.079 --> 00:30:36.079
So just a huge influence,

00:30:36.079 --> 00:30:37.079
and this for him personally,

00:30:37.079 --> 00:30:39.079
like this was a huge value for him,

00:30:39.079 --> 00:30:42.079
you know, relationships, relationships, real relationships.

00:30:42.079 --> 00:30:43.079
So being on staff and such,

00:30:43.079 --> 00:30:44.079
I had a chance to like, you know,

00:30:44.079 --> 00:30:46.079
kind of like press in a little bit,

00:30:46.079 --> 00:30:47.079
like because I was curious,

00:30:47.079 --> 00:30:48.079
again with the eyes of an outsider,

00:30:48.079 --> 00:30:50.079
like where is this, where is this coming from?

00:30:50.079 --> 00:30:53.079
What's the, what's the thing here, you know?

00:30:53.079 --> 00:30:56.079
And it was interesting as I talked with him,

00:30:56.079 --> 00:30:59.079
it was really, it was a cultural diagnosis

00:30:59.079 --> 00:31:02.079
that he could kind of just see

00:31:02.079 --> 00:31:06.079
that things unraveling relationally,

00:31:06.079 --> 00:31:08.079
you know, social bonds and ties

00:31:08.079 --> 00:31:10.079
kind of fraying in the culture.

00:31:10.079 --> 00:31:11.079
I mean, you think of it, for example,

00:31:11.079 --> 00:31:13.079
even at the level of like,

00:31:13.079 --> 00:31:15.079
not so much the nuclear family,

00:31:15.079 --> 00:31:16.079
I think if anything,

00:31:16.079 --> 00:31:18.079
we've kind of fallen back to that.

00:31:18.079 --> 00:31:20.079
We're like, that's everything.

00:31:20.079 --> 00:31:22.079
You know, so we're like,

00:31:22.079 --> 00:31:23.079
you kind of batten down the hatches

00:31:23.079 --> 00:31:25.079
with the nuclear family.

00:31:25.079 --> 00:31:27.079
But if you think of like the extended family,

00:31:27.079 --> 00:31:29.079
the way social ties have really frayed,

00:31:29.079 --> 00:31:30.079
you know what I mean?

00:31:30.079 --> 00:31:32.079
We don't tend to, I mean,

00:31:32.079 --> 00:31:34.079
some people are still around like,

00:31:34.079 --> 00:31:36.079
oh, their parents and grandparents

00:31:36.079 --> 00:31:37.079
and great-grandparents

00:31:37.079 --> 00:31:38.079
and everyone gets together like all the time

00:31:38.079 --> 00:31:40.079
and there's tons of social support.

00:31:40.079 --> 00:31:42.079
I mean, you can feel where kind of over,

00:31:42.079 --> 00:31:44.079
you know, the last few like kind of decades

00:31:44.079 --> 00:31:45.079
and really even centuries,

00:31:45.079 --> 00:31:47.079
that sort of fraying as people move here and there

00:31:47.079 --> 00:31:50.079
and, you know, it's kind of fraying.

00:31:50.079 --> 00:31:52.079
And even the way it's,

00:31:52.079 --> 00:31:54.079
he also kind of saw how

00:31:54.079 --> 00:31:57.079
it was putting tremendous stress on marriages

00:31:57.079 --> 00:32:00.079
as like that person

00:32:00.079 --> 00:32:04.079
became your like everything.

00:32:04.079 --> 00:32:05.079
You know what I mean?

00:32:05.079 --> 00:32:07.079
Your like one key relationship

00:32:07.079 --> 00:32:09.079
I can count on, hopefully.

00:32:09.079 --> 00:32:11.079
You know, and that,

00:32:11.079 --> 00:32:13.079
it's a lot of pressure to put on someone like,

00:32:13.079 --> 00:32:16.079
yes, I'll be your everything.

00:32:16.079 --> 00:32:19.079
Too much, you know, like too much.

00:32:19.079 --> 00:32:21.079
And again, add to this,

00:32:21.079 --> 00:32:22.079
he never mentioned this part,

00:32:22.079 --> 00:32:24.079
but also I just know in his story,

00:32:24.079 --> 00:32:25.079
he was a missionary for many years,

00:32:25.079 --> 00:32:27.079
like through his 20s.

00:32:27.079 --> 00:32:31.079
And I think something else he saw,

00:32:31.079 --> 00:32:32.079
he saw I think the culture

00:32:32.079 --> 00:32:35.079
with a little bit of an outsider's perspective.

00:32:35.079 --> 00:32:36.079
Because if you've ever done, you know,

00:32:36.079 --> 00:32:37.079
any sort of traveling or whatever,

00:32:37.079 --> 00:32:39.079
like when you go into other cultures,

00:32:39.079 --> 00:32:42.079
especially sort of non-Western cultures,

00:32:42.079 --> 00:32:44.079
but really almost anywhere outside of America,

00:32:44.079 --> 00:32:46.079
you immediately start to pick up on

00:32:46.079 --> 00:32:50.079
like the pretty intense social bonds

00:32:50.079 --> 00:32:52.079
cultures have, right?

00:32:52.079 --> 00:32:54.079
That's like deeply, deeply communal.

00:32:54.079 --> 00:32:57.079
It is not just about me as an individual

00:32:57.079 --> 00:32:58.079
doing my thing.

00:32:58.079 --> 00:32:59.079
You know, it's like, well, no, no, no.

00:32:59.079 --> 00:33:00.079
This is about we.

00:33:00.079 --> 00:33:01.079
This is about us.

00:33:01.079 --> 00:33:04.079
This, you know, really intense,

00:33:04.079 --> 00:33:06.079
yeah, kind of a togetherness.

00:33:06.079 --> 00:33:09.079
And I see that even in our neighbor

00:33:09.079 --> 00:33:10.079
lives to our left,

00:33:10.079 --> 00:33:13.079
and they are Guatemalan.

00:33:13.079 --> 00:33:14.079
I think immigrated through

00:33:14.079 --> 00:33:16.079
in their like teen years.

00:33:16.079 --> 00:33:17.079
And so now U.S. citizens,

00:33:17.079 --> 00:33:18.079
but really, you know,

00:33:18.079 --> 00:33:20.079
formative years in Guatemala.

00:33:20.079 --> 00:33:23.079
And like you see the difference

00:33:23.079 --> 00:33:25.079
like just in their culture

00:33:25.079 --> 00:33:27.079
and in their household.

00:33:27.079 --> 00:33:29.079
Like every weekend, there's like,

00:33:29.079 --> 00:33:30.079
maybe not every,

00:33:30.079 --> 00:33:31.079
but there's a lot of parties going on.

00:33:31.079 --> 00:33:32.079
I'll put it that way.

00:33:32.079 --> 00:33:34.079
And it's like everyone's about,

00:33:34.079 --> 00:33:36.079
it's not like five vehicles show up,

00:33:36.079 --> 00:33:38.079
you know, like, no, no, no, no.

00:33:38.079 --> 00:33:39.079
It's like, come on.

00:33:39.079 --> 00:33:41.079
I mean, even when we honestly barely knew them,

00:33:42.079 --> 00:33:43.079
they're like, come on over.

00:33:43.079 --> 00:33:44.079
Someone's having a birthday.

00:33:44.079 --> 00:33:46.079
Like, come on everyone.

00:33:46.079 --> 00:33:48.079
You know, that's just kind of the vibe,

00:33:48.079 --> 00:33:50.079
you know, as a culture.

00:33:50.079 --> 00:33:53.079
And again, contrast that with like

00:33:53.079 --> 00:33:56.079
American, America suburban living.

00:33:56.079 --> 00:33:58.079
You know, like, God forbid,

00:33:58.079 --> 00:34:01.079
your neighbor parks in the garage.

00:34:01.079 --> 00:34:03.079
You might never meet them.

00:34:03.079 --> 00:34:04.079
You know what I mean?

00:34:04.079 --> 00:34:06.079
Like, it's just pull in, pull out.

00:34:06.079 --> 00:34:07.079
It's like, oh, bye.

00:34:07.079 --> 00:34:08.080
And you kind of wave

00:34:08.080 --> 00:34:09.080
and then they pull out their Tesla or whatever.

00:34:09.080 --> 00:34:10.080
You know, like you never,

00:34:10.080 --> 00:34:11.080
you never see these people.

00:34:11.080 --> 00:34:13.080
And like, this is,

00:34:13.080 --> 00:34:15.080
this is kind of where, you know,

00:34:15.080 --> 00:34:18.080
the culture has gone.

00:34:18.080 --> 00:34:20.080
In 2020, the Surgeon General

00:34:20.080 --> 00:34:24.080
put out some statistics on like social connection.

00:34:24.080 --> 00:34:26.080
And obviously it was the pandemic partly

00:34:26.080 --> 00:34:27.080
that like spurred this on.

00:34:27.080 --> 00:34:29.080
And some of these graphs you can see,

00:34:29.080 --> 00:34:32.080
they kind of dip right there around 2020 pretty sharply.

00:34:32.080 --> 00:34:33.080
And I know it's small.

00:34:33.080 --> 00:34:34.080
The point is you don't have to,

00:34:34.080 --> 00:34:36.080
oh, kind of read through some of these stats.

00:34:36.080 --> 00:34:37.080
Really what I want you to notice

00:34:37.080 --> 00:34:39.080
is just the general trend lines

00:34:39.080 --> 00:34:42.080
in all but the first one.

00:34:42.080 --> 00:34:45.080
And that's because it's social isolation.

00:34:45.080 --> 00:34:46.080
So that's why it's up.

00:34:46.080 --> 00:34:47.080
You know, it's the,

00:34:47.080 --> 00:34:48.080
you're like, oh, that's good.

00:34:48.080 --> 00:34:49.080
Oh, no, never mind.

00:34:49.080 --> 00:34:50.080
Okay, social isolation is up.

00:34:50.080 --> 00:34:53.080
Everything else, household family engagements

00:34:53.080 --> 00:34:54.080
is just trending down.

00:34:54.080 --> 00:34:55.080
Here's some of these statistics.

00:34:55.080 --> 00:34:59.080
So people now spend an average of 24 hours per month,

00:34:59.080 --> 00:35:03.080
more hours per month alone than they used to.

00:35:03.080 --> 00:35:05.080
Oh, by the way, these are all since 2003.

00:35:05.080 --> 00:35:07.080
That's kind of the trend.

00:35:07.080 --> 00:35:09.080
Household family social engagement

00:35:09.080 --> 00:35:11.080
has declined about five hours a month.

00:35:11.080 --> 00:35:14.080
Companionship, which means sort of like shared leisure,

00:35:14.080 --> 00:35:17.080
that's decreased by 14 hours per month.

00:35:17.080 --> 00:35:18.080
Social engagements with friends

00:35:18.080 --> 00:35:21.080
has decreased 20 hours per month since 2003.

00:35:21.080 --> 00:35:22.080
Non-household family engagement

00:35:22.080 --> 00:35:24.080
has decreased 6 1⁄2 hours per month.

00:35:24.080 --> 00:35:25.080
Social engagement with others

00:35:25.080 --> 00:35:27.080
decreased 10 hours per month,

00:35:27.080 --> 00:35:30.080
and just kind of on and on and on.

00:35:30.080 --> 00:35:32.080
Also, this will surprise no one.

00:35:32.080 --> 00:35:33.080
People under age 35

00:35:33.080 --> 00:35:36.080
are trending towards spending more and more time alone.

00:35:36.080 --> 00:35:37.080
The number of young men right now

00:35:37.080 --> 00:35:40.080
who spend more than 8 hours a day alone

00:35:40.080 --> 00:35:44.080
has risen 20% since 2011.

00:35:44.080 --> 00:35:46.080
More than 40% of young women today

00:35:46.080 --> 00:35:49.080
report feeling lonely.

00:35:49.080 --> 00:35:51.080
40%!

00:35:51.080 --> 00:35:53.080
This is startling.

00:35:53.080 --> 00:35:54.080
And what's interesting, too,

00:35:54.080 --> 00:35:55.080
is these trends aren't like,

00:35:55.080 --> 00:35:57.080
well, everything kicked off in 2003.

00:35:57.080 --> 00:36:02.080
No, the trends have been going this way.

00:36:02.080 --> 00:36:04.080
Even from the 1970s to the 1990s,

00:36:04.080 --> 00:36:06.080
the frequency of folks

00:36:06.080 --> 00:36:10.080
just hosting parties and get-togethers

00:36:10.080 --> 00:36:12.080
and dinners and so on,

00:36:12.080 --> 00:36:15.080
that declined from the 1970s to the 1990s

00:36:15.080 --> 00:36:18.080
by 45% on average.

00:36:18.080 --> 00:36:19.080
And then it declined again

00:36:19.080 --> 00:36:21.080
from the early 2000s to today.

00:36:21.080 --> 00:36:23.080
So it was another 32%.

00:36:23.080 --> 00:36:25.080
So from the 1970s to today,

00:36:25.080 --> 00:36:28.080
you have a 77% decrease

00:36:28.080 --> 00:36:30.080
in folks just being like,

00:36:30.080 --> 00:36:32.080
hey, come on over, let's get together,

00:36:32.080 --> 00:36:36.080
whether a big party or just a small family.

00:36:36.080 --> 00:36:38.080
It's just trending down.

00:36:38.080 --> 00:36:41.080
So what's going on?

00:36:41.080 --> 00:36:43.080
What's going on here?

00:36:43.080 --> 00:36:44.080
What's up?

00:36:44.080 --> 00:36:46.080
Well, kind of in keeping with the series,

00:36:46.080 --> 00:36:49.080
I do think some of this is cultural.

00:36:49.080 --> 00:36:51.080
Some of this is the kind of grand social experiment

00:36:51.080 --> 00:36:53.080
that we've been undergoing.

00:36:53.080 --> 00:36:56.080
So we have, obviously, this strong,

00:36:56.080 --> 00:36:57.080
in the United States,

00:36:57.080 --> 00:37:00.080
we have this strong kind of individualist streak in us.

00:37:00.080 --> 00:37:01.080
The way we might sum it up is to say

00:37:01.080 --> 00:37:03.080
the United States really was founded

00:37:03.080 --> 00:37:05.080
on a view of the human being

00:37:05.080 --> 00:37:07.080
as a kind of individual, self-sufficient,

00:37:07.080 --> 00:37:10.080
rational, autonomous unit.

00:37:10.080 --> 00:37:13.080
And we don't necessarily use that language all the time,

00:37:13.080 --> 00:37:14.080
like, yes, I'm an American,

00:37:14.080 --> 00:37:15.080
I'm a rational, autonomous unit.

00:37:15.080 --> 00:37:17.080
I'm not saying we say that.

00:37:17.080 --> 00:37:19.080
It's more of just how we,

00:37:19.080 --> 00:37:21.080
almost kind of how our philosophy

00:37:21.080 --> 00:37:23.080
and social contracts and such,

00:37:23.080 --> 00:37:24.080
it's almost how it's...

00:37:24.080 --> 00:37:27.080
I mean, you can even see it in the legal system very much.

00:37:27.080 --> 00:37:29.080
People are very much treated as an individual.

00:37:29.080 --> 00:37:30.080
Which, again, I think that's probably,

00:37:30.080 --> 00:37:32.080
if there's an upside, that's it.

00:37:32.080 --> 00:37:34.080
Where I personally don't want to be judged

00:37:34.080 --> 00:37:36.080
for, like, the sins of my grandfather or something,

00:37:36.080 --> 00:37:37.080
or, like, thrown in prison

00:37:37.080 --> 00:37:40.080
because, well, your uncle was a political, you know,

00:37:40.080 --> 00:37:42.080
such and such, and so we're getting...

00:37:42.080 --> 00:37:44.080
Like, that's where you can see where the communal,

00:37:44.080 --> 00:37:45.080
at the political level,

00:37:45.080 --> 00:37:48.080
has its own kind of crazy and difficulties, you know.

00:37:48.080 --> 00:37:52.080
I think the challenge is when it's not just the legal setting,

00:37:52.080 --> 00:37:54.080
but it becomes a kind of cultural value,

00:37:54.080 --> 00:37:57.080
or almost a hyper-individualism

00:37:57.080 --> 00:37:59.080
that we just end up

00:37:59.080 --> 00:38:03.080
kind of being very cut off from one another.

00:38:05.080 --> 00:38:08.080
Yeah, now you combine that view of the human being

00:38:08.080 --> 00:38:11.080
and the social settings it creates,

00:38:11.080 --> 00:38:12.080
as we talked about earlier,

00:38:12.080 --> 00:38:14.080
with the suburban homes,

00:38:14.080 --> 00:38:16.080
all kind of not really facing one another,

00:38:16.080 --> 00:38:18.080
you know, I mean, there's no common courtyards,

00:38:18.080 --> 00:38:19.080
or, like, no, no, no,

00:38:19.080 --> 00:38:21.080
everyone's kind of facing alone

00:38:21.080 --> 00:38:22.080
and pulling in their garages,

00:38:22.080 --> 00:38:24.080
and, you know, okay, so you get that,

00:38:24.080 --> 00:38:27.080
and then you combine that

00:38:27.080 --> 00:38:31.080
with the rise of technology, right?

00:38:31.080 --> 00:38:33.080
The rise of, of course, television,

00:38:33.080 --> 00:38:36.080
and then smartphones and social media,

00:38:36.080 --> 00:38:38.080
and a dozen or so streaming services,

00:38:38.080 --> 00:38:40.080
and, you know, what happens?

00:38:40.080 --> 00:38:42.080
Well, you know, surprise, surprise,

00:38:42.080 --> 00:38:44.080
people were kind of increasing,

00:38:44.080 --> 00:38:46.080
you take that value with that technology,

00:38:46.080 --> 00:38:48.080
and what you get is kind of,

00:38:48.080 --> 00:38:50.080
well, people are just increasingly

00:38:50.080 --> 00:38:52.080
spending more and more time alone, right?

00:38:52.080 --> 00:38:55.080
As we kind of turn to the virtual,

00:38:55.080 --> 00:38:57.080
to the technological,

00:38:57.080 --> 00:39:00.080
to kind of fill our leisure time.

00:39:00.080 --> 00:39:03.080
And my hunch is,

00:39:03.080 --> 00:39:04.080
